    ('transportation-priorities', 1, 'Beaty championed the Beaverton Downtown Loop — a 1.3-mile downtown circuit with protected bike lanes, wider sidewalks, pedestrian crossing treatments, and enhanced bus stops — securing a $2 million federal DOT grant. She approved Resolution No. 4838 supporting climate-friendly transportation, developed a full Active Transportation Plan, and endorsed bus rapid transit as the locally preferred alternative for the TV Highway corridor. She testified against state transportation-bill cuts that would reduce city funding for these projects. Transportation investment is explicitly framed around safety, equity, and climate. This maps to Chair 1 (prioritize pedestrian infrastructure, cycling networks, and public transit).', ARRAY['https://bonamici.house.gov/media/press-releases/bonamici-wyden-merkley-announce-funding-beaverton-road-safety-improvements', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WXlzylLa-nM', 'https://beavertonoregon.gov/410/Active-Transportation-Plan', 'https://medium.com/usmayors/meet-the-mayor-lacey-beaty-5ad15bc11951']::text[]),
    ('climate-change', 3, 'Beaty established the Beaverton Climate Action Task Force (Resolution No. 2712), presided over adoption of a Climate Action Plan targeting net-zero community emissions by 2050 and city-government carbon neutrality by 2030, created a city sustainability department, and invested in renewables (hydropower on the water system). In her 2020 candidate interview she called for state-level cap-and-invest policy. She called the climate crisis one of her top three priorities. This is an active, multi-pronged clean-energy investment posture that maps to Chair 3 (invest in clean energy while gradually reducing reliance on fossil fuels).', ARRAY['https://www.buildreuse.org/community-highlights/beaverton-or-climate-action-plan', 'https://blog.energytrust.org/city-of-beaverton-and-energy-trust-celebrate-hydropower-success/', 'https://washcodems.org/2020/04/26/lacey-beaty-candidate-interview/', 'https://www.climatesolutions.org/beaverton-or']::text[]),
    ('local-environment', 3, 'Beaty stated in August 2023 that ''while growth is important, this should be balanced with sustainability,'' specifically citing the city''s Purple Pipe reclaimed-water system as a sustainability infrastructure investment enabling growth. The city applies environmental standards to development (composting, EV infrastructure, tree canopy lists) while still actively pursuing growth and development. This balanced stance — consistent environmental standards with reasonable implementation flexibility — maps to Chair 3.', ARRAY['https://blog.energytrust.org/with-small-steps-beaverton-is-leading-the-way-in-climate-action/', 'https://medium.com/usmayors/meet-the-mayor-lacey-beaty-5ad15bc11951']::text[]),
    ('public-safety-approach', 3, 'Beaty explicitly called the #8CantWait use-of-force reforms the ''bare minimum'' and advocated police reform as a councilor. The city maintained a Mental Health Resource Team (police-clinician co-responder model). However, she did not support defunding: Beaverton increased its police budget under her watch and budget cuts in 2025 were fiscal-driven (structural deficit), not ideological. She supported keeping current public safety funding while adding mental health crisis response capacity. This maps to Chair 3 (keep current public safety funding while adding crisis response teams).', ARRAY['https://www.beavertonvalleytimes.com/news/beavertons-beaty-isnt-backing-away-from-police-reform/article_5c1d7373-ec1e-5e71-802b-6cac124c7585.html', 'https://beavertonpolice.org/198/Mental-Health-Response-Team', 'https://katu.com/news/local/beaverton-faces-107-million-budget-shortfall-plans-cuts-to-police-and-public-works-library-street-repair-resident-portland-oregon-washington-county-finances-economy-consumer']::text[]),
    ('civil-rights', 2, 'Beaty signed the U.S. Conference of Mayors'' Compact on Racial Equity (January 2022), which explicitly affirms that systemic discrimination exists and commits signatories to work to eliminate inequities in housing, wages, education, and criminal justice. Her administration developed a Downtown Equity Strategy targeting a Central Beaverton neighborhood historically harmed by redlining, and maintained an Office of Equity & Inclusion. This record of affirming systemic discrimination and taking governmental action to address it maps to Chair 2 (strengthen civil rights enforcement and address systemic discrimination).', ARRAY['https://www.usmayors.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/COMPACT-ON-RACIAL-EQUITY-011922.docx.pdf', 'https://beavertonoregon.gov/385/Cultural-Inclusion', 'https://medium.com/usmayors/meet-the-mayor-lacey-beaty-5ad15bc11951']::text[]),
    ('local-immigration', 1, 'Beaverton''s city council approved a strengthened sanctuary city ordinance on January 20, 2026, adding language that prohibits city employees from providing immigration-status information to federal agencies for immigration enforcement purposes (except where legally required) and banning use of city personnel to arrest or detain anyone for immigration enforcement. Beaty supported the ordinance and stated that residents would live in fear if local law enforcement targeted them for deportation. The council also considered amending city code to allow officers to investigate and cite ICE agents for local law violations. This matches Chair 1 (refuse ICE detainers; prohibit city employees from sharing immigration status information with federal agencies).', ARRAY['https://beavertonvalleytimes.com/2026/01/27/beaverton-hones-sanctuary-city-code-preventing-city-employees-from-helping-ice-officials/', 'https://katu.com/news/local/beaverton-mayor-lacey-beaty-councilors-nadia-hasan-kevin-teater-considers-having-officers-investigate-federal-immigration-ice-agents', 'https://www.koin.com/news/portland-beaverton-to-remain-sanctuary-cities/']::text[]),
